{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793183542,"nonce":"aTI2d4BHrYVTDNTtGe8iR5cHcOM=","title":"Recover lost local MSB VMs from fenced persistent workspace and document volumes","message":"The current runner uses guest-owned volumes deleted with the VM. Keep workspace/Git data and private document state in runner-owned persistent named disks so a lost VM can be replaced without recloning. Bind disks and toolchain identity in the durable registry, rotate generation/transport credentials and close stale connections before replacement, verify the same native document identity before Ready, and refuse missing/foreign storage instead of creating a new baseline. Explicit cloud-copy deletion must confirm both guest and disk cleanup before releasing admission. Keep legacy workspaces fenced until deliberately migrated.\n\nPersist hosted journal updates privately before returning their delta; write-ahead graph/disk baselines and filesystem durability barriers must preserve edits acknowledged immediately before VM loss. Exercise deletion-only edits, unsaved text, source/binary files, Git index/refs, owner isolation, duplicate concurrent opens, failed recovery and cleanup on real NUC MSB.\n\nThis local surviving-volume recovery is separate from full host/disk loss recovery through Durable Objects/R2. Keep cross-host automaticRestore false until full manifests, stable document identities, external-write reconciliation and distributed leases are complete. Parents: 52138fcda83502ec87679713955f7cf7c7f7e8654a0ef55ba60357bd0f22a904 and 529764c1f9d8a4aa4fd6b9b203165dd4df383e332f1a4cbf110441de29bcaec2.","files":null}]}